","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"O6CqbHBPmN"}],"key":"HFwZOERvhp"},{"type":"text","value":" Linearizing the stimulus variable like this may feel like cheating and in some sense it is. The fit of the type 1 model will be trivially better if the stimulus variable was first transformed by an identical type 1 model. Indeed, following such preprocessing, the single-subject AIC / BIC values of ReMeta models cannot (easily) be compared to other models, as some parameters were used during preprocessing, even if only at the group level.","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"mVVimTMuwQ"}],"key":"Ud03auVZ3a"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The advantage of careful linearization – as outlined above – is that it likely improves parameter estimates across both the type 1 and type 2 stage. At the type 1 stage, ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"V7fUTeOXMM"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\sigma_1","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">σ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"hpTJDgAcD8"},{"type":"text","value":" / ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"ItQH32HSU6"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type1_noise","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"JSVeFgqu3g"},{"type":"text","value":" now provides a single and robust estimate of an observer’s overall sensitivity, independent of stimulus difficulty levels. At the type 2 stage, forwarded type 1 decision values are more precise and provide a better basis for type 2 parameter inference.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"spJPAUNvRC"}],"key":"IiZEnJTn9r"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It can be thus a trade-off between (likely) better parameter inference and AIC / BIC values that can be compared to other models. If you do care about model comparison, your options are to either i) do stimulus linearization based on an independent dataset (or pilot subjects / excluded subjects), ii) ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"XjthpbE3pf"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"not use","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"nI2FBNTK94"}],"key":"lsKaocoDg0"},{"type":"text","value":" this linearization method and instead provide stimulus magnitude in a form that approximates a signal-to-noise ratio (using knowledge about the process of stimulus generation), or iii) just not care about linearization at all.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"oGYmeEDRGV"}],"key":"tzlCiHzx1V"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The alternative – adopted by most other models – is to estimate a sensitivity parameter for each participant and each difficulty level separately. While this allows for the computation of a clean per-participant likelihood, it comes with disadvantages:","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"gs9cqFykQ7"}],"key":"jJT8yhUKOm"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sensitivity estimated in this way conflates the difficulty of the stimulus and the specific sensitivity of the participant.","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"MxNAI2eg6q"}],"key":"E8Qs4DeWon"}],"key":"jqk60AgvyW"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It requires enough samples per participant and difficulty level, as otherwise parameter fits become quickly unstable.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"ToHm4GWBsA"}],"key":"xhNAMNmnz7"}],"key":"yCAgGDyCdj"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is unclear how to fit these models if stimulus evidence is continuous and not restricted to a few discrete evidence levels.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"tse4otwiBR"}],"key":"Zrd9VAwohZ"}],"key":"AMKiqLnv78"}],"key":"ydLn4hcgVO"}],"key":"HiZnZqiDBc"